%I A397343 #80 Sep 19 2026 20:43:56
%S A397343 19,28,68,82,86,91,100,109,129,133,139,167,176,188,190,192,193,208,
%T A397343 219,280,291,313,319,331,338,368,379,383,386,391,397,446,464,469,478,
%U A397343 487,496,556,565,608,617,638,644,649,655,671,680,683,694,716,739,748,761,784,793,802,806
%N A397343 Happy numbers whose trajectories under iteration of the sum of squares of digits map to 100 before going to the value of 1.
%C A397343 Powers of 10 are the only numbers that map to 1 in one iteration of the sum of squares of digits mapping. [Edited by _Jianing Song_, Sep 09 2026]
%C A397343 Infinite since it contains the subfamily 68*10^k for k >= 0 (among all numbers with one 6, one 8, and all other digits 0). - _Michael S. Branicky_, Sep 06 2026
%H A397343 Amiram Eldar, <a href="/A397343/b397343.txt">Table of n, a(n) for n = 1..10000</a>
%H A397343 Wikipedia, <a href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Happy_number">Happy number</a>.
%e A397343 28 is a term since 28 -> 2^2 + 8^2 = 68 -> 6^2 + 8^2 = 100 -> 1^2 + 0^2 + 0^2 = 1.
%e A397343 13 is not a term since 13 -> 1^2 + 3^2 = 10 -> 1^2 + 0^2 = 1.
%e A397343 2 is not a term since it is not happy (cf. A000216).
%t A397343 q[n_] := MemberQ[NestWhileList[Plus @@ (IntegerDigits[#]^2) &, n, UnsameQ, All], 100]; Select[Range[1000], q] (* _Amiram Eldar_, Sep 04 2026 *)
%o A397343 (PARI) isA397343(n) = while(n!=1 && n!=4 && n!=100, n=A003132(n)); return(n==100) \\ _Jianing Song_, Sep 06 2026; see A003132 for its program
%Y A397343 Subsequence of A007770.
%Y A397343 Cf. A000216, A003132, A399577.
%K A397343 nonn,base,easy,new
%O A397343 1,1
